1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,080
 Hi, in this last one I will talk about approaches to build an app within AI editor.

2
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:12,880
 Let's get started. Before you begin building a complete application with AI,

3
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:19,360
 it's important to decide how you want to work. A good approach helps you move faster,

4
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:22,800
 avoid mistakes and stay in control of your final result.

5
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:27,920
 Different projects and situations call for different workflows.

6
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:34,800
 In this last one, you'll learn four main approaches, and when each of them is the best choice.

7
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:37,280
 Let's start with the step-by-step approach.

8
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:43,520
 In this method, you work with the AI by giving one shot task at a time.

9
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:49,840
 You review every update before moving to the next one, so you keep maximum control and

10
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:56,400
 minimize risk. For example, you could ask the AI to create a vid, react, TypeScript app

11
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:03,360
 named calculator. Then add a calculator component with a display and a 4x5 keypad

12
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:07,120
 containing all the buttons for digits, operations and others.

13
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:13,920
 Next, request simple CSS for a clean design. A white card with rounded corners,

14
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:20,800
 a soft shadow and centered content. Finally, implement input validation to prevent multiple

15
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:26,720
 decimals handle divide by zero safely and define proper clear and backspace behavior.

16
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:33,440
 The advantages of this approach are full control, complete transparency and very low risk of

17
00:01:33,440 --> 00:01:38,000
 breaking things. It's also easy to review and fix small errors immediately.

18
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:43,520
 The downsides of slower progress, many problems and the chance of losing direction if you don't

19
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:50,400
 have a plan. Use this approach for careful updates in real projects, hot fixes, small improvements,

20
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:56,720
 or refactoring one screen of feature at a time. Now let's move to the design first or UI driven

21
00:01:56,720 --> 00:02:04,320
 approach. Here, you upload a screenshot or describe the layout and the AI builds the interface first.

22
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:10,880
 This is great for quick visual results or when design matters more than functionality early on.

23
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:17,040
 For example, you might ask the AI to build your apps user interface from a screenshot

24
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:23,360
 using VIT plus React plus TypeScript. You could tell it to keep the CSS simple,

25
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:27,840
 include flags in the selects and make a clean centered card layout.

26
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:34,320
 The pros of this approach are fast visual progress and easy matching to a specific design.

27
00:02:34,320 --> 00:02:41,200
 The cons of that logic is added later which might lead to rework and without clear direction,

28
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:48,480
 the AI might structure code poorly. This approach is best for converting Figma or screenshots

29
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:56,400
 into code rapid demos, prototypes, landing pages or marketing pages. Next is the plan first approach.

30
00:02:56,400 --> 00:03:03,760
 With this method, you start by creating a detailed structured plan with the AI before writing any

31
00:03:03,760 --> 00:03:09,200
 code. Once you approve the plans, the AI builds the project according to that structure.

32
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:15,840
 For example, you can ask the AI to create a step-by-step plan to build a calculator app using

33
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:23,520
 VIT plus React plus TypeScript. The app should display and a 4x5 keypad containing all the

34
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:30,320
 buttons for digits, operations and others. The advantages are a clear structure from the beginning,

35
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:38,400
 fewer mistakes and better quality results. It's especially good for projects with multiple

36
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:45,760
 components or files. The disadvantages are longer preparation time and the need for detailed

37
00:03:45,760 --> 00:03:51,280
 instructions to get an accurate plan. This approach works best when starting a new app,

38
00:03:51,280 --> 00:03:58,320
 building large features or working on UI, logic and tests together. Finally, let's talk

39
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:05,520
 about the mixed approach. This combines planning, design and detailed steps for a balanced workflow.

40
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:12,800
 You start by providing both a screenshot and your functional requirements. Then ask the AI to create

41
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:19,440
 a plan that merges them. This gives you the speed of the design first approach and the safety

42
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:25,920
 of the plan first method. For example, you might ask the AI to create a step-by-step plan to build

43
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:33,040
 your app from a screenshot using VIT plus React plus TypeScript. As the app should display

44
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:40,800
 and a 4x5 keypad containing all the buttons for digits, operations and others. Also keep the CSS

45
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:47,200
 simple and user clean centered card layout. The mixed approach offers a great balance between

46
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:54,000
 speed and structure. With less rework, then UI only methods and strong control with visual clarity.

47
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:01,280
 Its downsides are that it takes more steps than design first and still requires reviews at each stage.

48
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:09,200
 It's ideal when your UI is ready, but logic is also important. For features that touch multiple

49
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:16,080
 parts of the app or when you want reliable results quickly. Final comparison, step-by-step gives

50
00:05:16,080 --> 00:05:22,640
 maximum safety but moves slowly. If you need total control, choose step-by-step. Design first

51
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:29,280
 is fast and visually impressive, but may require later fixes. If you want fast visible progress,

52
00:05:29,280 --> 00:05:35,200
 go with design first. Plant first builds a clean, organized project,

53
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:41,520
 but takes more setup time. If you prefer well-structured, maintainable code, use plan first.

54
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:48,720
 Mixed combines fast visuals with solid structure for the best balance. If you want both speed

55
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:52,880
 and reliability, mixed is your best choice. All that, let's wrap up.

56
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:59,200
 Choosing the right approach early makes development smoother, faster, and cleaner.

57
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:06,560
 Pick the method that fits your design status, features scope, and how much control you want.

58
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:11,040
 You can even combine approaches in a single project to balance speed and quality.

59
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:17,120
 Good workflow choice means faster delivery and better code quality.

60
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:22,240
 I hope this lesson was useful for you and see you in the next lesson.

